I am writing a vercel serverless function. When I try to call it in my browser, I get an error in my terminal saying "cannot use import statement outside a module". Please offer solutions.|

import type { VercelRequest, VercelResponse } from '@vercel/node';

export default function handler(
  request: VercelRequest,
  response: VercelResponse,
) {
  response.status(200).json({
    body: request.body,
    query: request.query,
    cookies: request.cookies,
  });

}

The error "cannot use import statement outside a module" is caused by the fact that you are trying to use an ES module-level import statement in a non-module function. To fix this, follow these steps:

1.   First, make sure that you are using Vercel's `` @vercel/node `` package, which supports ES module syntax.
2.   Next, convert your function to an ES module by wrapping it in a `` default `` export statement:


```javascript
import type { VercelRequest, VercelResponse } from '@vercel/node';

export default function handler(
  request: VercelRequest,
  response: VercelResponse,
) {
  response.status(200).json({
    body: request.body,
    query: request.query,
    cookies: request.cookies,
  });
}

```
<ol start="3"><li>Then, make sure that you are calling your function from a file that also uses ES module syntax, such as an ES module file or an ES module-enabled script.</li><li>Finally, make sure that you are using the correct import syntax for your target environment. If you are using ES module syntax in your browser, you need to use <code>import</code> instead of <code>require</code>. If you are using ES module syntax in your server environment, you can use either <code>import</code> or <code>require</code> depending on your preference.</li></ol>

